# 6. M2 — Memfd Lifecycle Tracking

## 6.1 Objective

M2 asks:

**What happened to the same memfd object after creation?**

M1 establishes object creation identity.

M2 extends this into object lifecycle and provenance tracking.

Questions include:

- Which descriptors refer to the object?
- Was the descriptor duplicated?
- Was it inherited by another process?
- Was the object reopened through `/proc/self/fd/`?
- Was the object modified?
- Did it remain alive after an individual descriptor closed?
- Was it used as an execution source?
- When was the backing object finally destroyed?

## 6.2 The crucial identity distinction

This was one of the most important architectural discoveries in the project.

Do not treat the following entities as interchangeable.

| Entity | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Memfd backing object | Underlying anonymous file/inode |
| Open file description | `struct file` instance |
| Descriptor reference | Descriptor table and FD slot |

Consider:

```text
Process A
  FD 3
    |
    v
  struct file F1
    |
    v
  Inode I1
```

After `dup()`:

```text
Process A
  FD 3 ----+
           |
           v
        struct file F1
           |
  FD 7 ----+
           |
           v
         Inode I1
```

The descriptors refer to the same open file description.

After reopening through `/proc/self/fd/3`:

```text
Process A
  FD 3
    |
    v
  struct file F1
    |
    +--------+
             |
             v
           Inode I1
             ^
             |
  struct file F2
    |
    v
  FD 8
```

Here F1 and F2 are distinct `struct file` instances, but they refer to the same backing inode.

This means:

- A registry keyed only by FD is insufficient.
- A registry keyed only by `struct file *` is insufficient.
- A registry keyed only by inode number is unsafe because inode numbers can be reused and are not globally unique.

## 6.3 Approved two-level registry direction

The chosen conceptual architecture is:

```text
Open-file registry:
    struct file * → open-file mapping → object token

Memfd-object registry:
    backing-object identity → object token + lifecycle state
```

The backing-object registry is inode-anchored.

The open-file registry provides fast lookup from an observed `struct file *` to the corresponding object token.

The object token is an opaque, kernel-generated monotonically increasing identity.

Kernel pointers remain internal map keys.

The inode number and device information can be retained as metadata, but must not be the sole object identity.

